EXT. CITY STREET - FOOD TRUCK - DAY

A white food truck is parked near the old market. The sign says BIG DAVE'S TACOS. A small line of customers waits in the sun. The smell of onions and hot oil is in the air.

INT. FOOD TRUCK - CONTINUOUS

Narrow and hot. BIG DAVE, 50s, wide shoulders, works the grill. JEN, 30s, takes orders at the window. MO, 20s, is cutting limes and does not talk much. SAM, 30s, a quiet man in a clean jacket, sits on a milk crate near the back with a paper cup of coffee.

JEN
(to everyone)
Okay, team. Three pork, two fish, one no onions. Mo, the no-onions one is for the old lady.

MO
(not looking up)
Okay.

BIG DAVE
(flipping meat)
The old lady always want extra sauce. She says it is for her health.

JEN
(laughing)
Dave, her health is fine. She just likes sauce.

Sam lifts his cup. He watches the street. A grey car is parked across the road for the second day.

SAM
(quietly, to himself)
Same car. Same man.

JEN
(over her shoulder)
Sam, you say something?

SAM
No. Nothing. I talk to the coffee.

BIG DAVE
(smiling)
The coffee is the best listener in the truck.

A phone BUZZES on the shelf above the grill. Big Dave wipes his hands on his apron and picks it up.

BIG DAVE (CONT'D)
(into the phone)
Hello? Yes. Yes, this is Dave.

MRS. K (V.O.)
(phone, crackling, warm)
David, it is Mrs. Kowalczyk from the corner. Is my delivery ready? My grandson is coming at one o'clock and he is very hungry.

BIG DAVE
(into the phone)
Yes, Mrs. K. Twelve tacos, wrapped, in the back. Mo will bring them in ten minutes.

MRS. K (V.O.)
(phone)
You are a good boy. Tell Jen she needs a husband.

JEN
(loudly, from the window)
I heard that!

Big Dave puts the phone down, smiling. Mo slides the limes into a plastic tub.

MO
(quietly, to Sam)
You are looking at the car again.

SAM
(beat)
Maybe I am.

MO
(shrugging)
Is not our business. We make tacos.

SAM
(looking at him)
You are right. We make tacos.

A customer, a TEENAGE GIRL in headphones, steps to the window and puts down a ten-dollar bill.

JEN
(to the girl)
Hi. What can I make for you?

TEENAGE GIRL
Just two. Anything.

JEN
(over her shoulder)
Two pork, Dave!

BIG DAVE
(to the grill, to himself)
Two pork. Always pork. Nobody is brave anymore.

Outside, the grey car's door opens. A man in a brown coat gets out and crosses the road slowly, hands in his pockets.

SAM
(standing, setting down his cup, soft)
Everyone. Stay calm. Keep working.

JEN
(low)
What is happening?

SAM
(not turning)
Maybe nothing. Maybe a man wants a taco.

The man arrives at the window. He looks past Jen, past the grill, and finds Sam.

THE MAN IN THE BROWN COAT
Hello, Sam. It has been a long time.

SAM
(a pause, level)
Yes. A long time, Inspector.

BIG DAVE
(to the grill, muttering)
Inspector. Of course. The one day I forget the napkins.

JEN
(whispering, to Mo)
Is this a joke?

MO
(whispering)
I think no.

The man puts a business card on the counter. Jen reads it without touching it.

THE MAN IN THE BROWN COAT
Do not worry. I am not here for the truck. I only wish to talk. Five minutes. Then I eat, and then I go.

SAM
(taking off his apron, folding it neatly)
Dave. Please keep my coffee warm.

BIG DAVE
(still not turning)
The coffee will wait. It is a patient coffee.

Sam steps out of the back door. The man in the brown coat holds it for him. They walk down the street together, slowly, the way old friends walk, or old enemies.

JEN
(to Mo, to Dave, to the air)
Okay. Does anyone know what just happened?

MO
(cutting a lime)
No.

BIG DAVE
(flipping the pork)
Two pork for the girl. Then we will see.

Jen sighs and wipes the counter. The line shuffles forward. The sun keeps shining, and the onions keep sizzling, as if nothing in the world had changed at all.

A phone BUZZES again on the shelf. Nobody picks it up.
